# Day 17 puzzle desciption gives no test input, so these are hand-crafted intcode programs (read: AI). They
# emit an ASCII camera grid (`104, <ascii>` pairs, `#` scaffold, `.` empty, `^`/`v`/`<`/`>` vacuum robot,
# rows split by `\n`) and then either halt (part 1) or feed the movement routine and report on it (part 2).
#
# Part 1: intersections are scaffold tiles with more than 2 scaffold neighbours, so the alignment sum is the
# sum of x * y over those tiles. TEST_STRING_1 is a plus-cross (one intersection, answer 1), TEST_STRING_2 a
# simple corner (no intersection, answer 0) and TEST_STRING_3 a T-junction (three-neighbour tile, answer 2).
#
# Part 2 programs are padded with a harmless `2,0,0,999` multiply head because get_p2 overwrites memory
# position 0 with 2 (vacuum mode) before running. Their route must be reproducible by the walker and their
# extra tail consumes the routine that the solver feeds it:
# - TEST_STRING_4 has a compressible route (main routine `A,B,A,B,C` with `L,4`/`R,6,L,10`/`R,12`); its tail sums
#   the ascii value of every routine byte and outputs the running total, so part 2 equals the exact checksum
#   of the byte stream the solver sends (1503).
# - TEST_STRING_5 uses 17 distinct step counts that cannot be reduced to A/B/C functions of at most 5 steps
#   each, so the solver falls back to an empty routine (only the newline and `n` responses are sent), whose
#   checksum is 160.
# - TEST_STRING_6 reuses the TEST_STRING_4 maze but knits the first two routine bytes together and then halts,
#   covering the halt path of the part 2 output loop (answer 109).

